WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.230
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.830
My name is Priya Natarajan

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.040
and I'm the current director
of the Franke Program

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.550
in Science and the Humanities at Yale.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.300
I welcome you all warmly to
the final spring semester event

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.620
this afternoon.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.890
We have a lot of exciting new
events planned for this fall,

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.160
including a talk and musical performance

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.670
by the internationally acclaimed
jazz composer, performer,

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.420
and former Yale College
physics undergraduate alum,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.070
as it turns out, Vijay Iyer.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.570
I would also like to
remind you to contribute

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.580
to our ongoing Learning by Doing Project

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.810
and submit videos of
do-at-home science projects

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.133
to add to our bank.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.160
Anyway, coming back to today,
we have an exciting talk

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.030
by one of our own
brilliant young scholars,

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.670
a Franke fellow, Dr. Ana Florence.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.810
First, I would like to start
by recognizing our benefactors,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.840
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.690
Their generosity is what makes

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:06.410
this flagship interdisciplinary
program at Yale possible.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.543
I'm delighted that Mrs.
Franke has joined us today.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.420
And I want to remind all
of you who are assembled

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.410
that we are recording this event,

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.400
and all participants will
therefore have their videos muted

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.250
for the duration of the talk,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.480
and any questions that you might have

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.360
that arise during the talk
maybe submitted at any time

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.743
in the Q&amp;A feature.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.880
Nearly five years ago, when
I took over as the director

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.109
of the Franke Program, and
had a wonderful long coffee

00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:42.460
with Rich Franke, one of
the first things I told him

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.740
that I wanted to do was
to expand the footprint

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.890
of the extremely successful
Franke Fellowship

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.690
and extend it to graduate students

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.000
and postdoctoral scholars at Yale.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.030
He was incredibly enthusiastic and excited

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:00.210
at the possibility of
extending the support

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.160
for interdisciplinary
projects across the board

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.420
to many more Yalies.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.570
I am utterly delighted to introduce

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.310
one of our recent
postdoctoral Franke fellows,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.400
Dr. Ana Florence, as our speaker today.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.630
The title of her talk

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.340
is Social and Structural
Contours of Marginalization

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.710
in the Context of COVID-19.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.810
So Ana is amongst our
cohort of graduate student

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.900
and postdoctoral scholars

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.350
who get Franke Fellowship support money

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.420
to seed ambitious, bold ideas

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.990
that cross disciplinary boundaries

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.733
during their time at Yale.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.150
Dr. Ana Florence, who is a
recent postdoctoral fellow

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.790
from the Franke Program,
is a clinical psychologist

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.510
who is originally from
Brazil, with experience

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.760
in the institutionalization
and implementation

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.070
of public community-based
mental health services.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:03.260
Her scholarship focuses on
the open dialogue approach

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.510
and other dialogic practices

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.380
for people experiencing psychosis.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:13.180
And she has worked on
human rights and the role

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.690
of lived experience in
transforming systems practices

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.800
and conceptions of mental health.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.200
She has recently joined
Columbia University

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.263
as a postdoctoral fellow and
also works with the OnTrackNY,

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.930
engaging youth and young adults

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.220
in research and quality improvement

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.963
to support a learning healthcare
system in New York City.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.630
For her Franke project, and
this is what really attracted us

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:42.480
to her work and alerted us
to the talent that she brings

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.330
to this wonderful project,

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.030
she aimed to address the
current understanding

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.500
of the mental health impacts
of COVID-19 pandemic.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.470
It was an extremely
timely and urgent project.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:57.170
This unprecedented public health crisis,

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.140
she claimed in her proposal,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.190
begs a comprehensive understanding

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.200
of the social, economic, psychological,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.040
and political underpinnings of suffering.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.720
Through a strong multidisciplinary focus,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.410
she suggested that she would look

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:14.810
at ways to expand the ways
in which mental health impact

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.530
of COVID-19 is conceptualized,

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.240
inviting the voices of those

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:22.230
who have gone through mental
health challenges in the past,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.290
and have accrued valuable wisdom

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.263
in their recovery journey to the fore.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.260
She offered to hold a
series of consensus groups

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.910
guided by her previous
research on this topic

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.980
to develop this new conceptual framework.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.710
Broadening our
understanding of this crisis

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.550
to include the social dimension
of experiences of suffering

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.390
can inform public policy
and future research

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.330
to better respond collectively
to this global emergency.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.930
So I was just reading
out of her wonderful,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:56.010
super compelling proposal that
we got a couple of years ago,

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.900
and I'm delighted to tell you

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.200
that Ana will be talking to us today

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.400
about this Wisdom Project,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.860
this project, which was born in the wake

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.260
of the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.960
And what she has done and
will be talking to us today

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.220
is to reacquaint us
with a group of persons

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.530
with lived experience with
mental health problems,

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:23.250
and for whom she has produced
a series of webinars,

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.280
with whom, for whom she has
produced a series of webinars,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.480
podcasts, and conducted research

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.250
on how we can collectively make sense

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:34.940
of these hugely disruptive phenomenon.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.710
This pandemic, far from
being a great equalizer,

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.100
which is what we all
originally thought, right,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.810
has brought to fore the issues

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.230
that are at the very core of
our very inequitable societies

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.200
that we can no longer ignore,

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.820
systemic racism, oppression,
poverty, and health inequities,

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.350
and access to healthcare inequities.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.520
Her talk today will focus on a series

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.260
of regional conversations
that invited the voices

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.950
and experiences of those
who have endured firsthand

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.760
what it's like to survive
precarious livelihoods,

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.360
racism, oppression, marginalization,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.770
stigma, and other unfreedoms.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.270
We are excited and utterly delighted

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.920
to have you speak to us today, Ana.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.140
And we really look forward to hearing more

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.150
about this wonderful Franke project.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:25.280
Ana, take it away.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.100
- Thank you so much, Priya.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.800
That was a wonderful introduction.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.710
I'm just gonna start sharing my screen

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.233
and we'll ask someone to confirm
if you can see my slides.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.546
- [Woman] Looks great.

00:06:37.546 --> 00:06:39.290
- Perfect, thank you so much.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.320
So I would first like to
start by also thanking

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.750
the Franke Program for
the support of this work.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.090
I don't think this work could
ever have happened without it.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:53.690
And as Priya was mentioning,
it aligns so well

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.920
with the mission of the Franke Program

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.030
in Science and the Humanities at Yale,

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:05.030
and I think one of the
pieces that really fit well

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.050
with the work that I was
already previously doing

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:12.210
is the interdisciplinary piece.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.610
And so, I really wanna start by saying

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.690
that I didn't do this work alone,

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.010
and by centering my
colleagues and everyone

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.780
that was involved through these past,

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:26.343
I'd say two years this
research has been happening.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:31.410
And I really wanted to also
say that we were a team

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.330
and a collective of
scholars and activists,

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:37.170
and we came from various
different backgrounds,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:41.480
from psychologists, to
sociologists, to historians.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.900
And we came from different countries.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.760
Folks who participated were
based in Brazil, and Canada,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.413
and here in the United States.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:54.550
And one important feature
of this entire project

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.670
that I would like to
center is that a number

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:01.840
of participants of the core team

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.170
were people who had firsthand experience

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.250
of mental health challenges, or madness,

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:12.250
or extreme states, or however
people want to conceptualize

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.460
and identify as.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.010
And I think that this was a core

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:20.780
and very important feature of this group.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:24.900
And also, the work that we
did in the various engagements

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.680
attempted to be as
participatory as we could.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:32.600
And I think we can claim here and say that

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.480
our work was actually led by people

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.230
who had firsthand or direct experience

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:39.973
with the mental health system.

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:44.900
So with that, I'd like to start by saying

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.740
a little bit about what
this Wisdom Project was

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:52.640
and how it emerged.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.450
So initially, once the pandemic started,

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:56.800
we were all doing research.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.270
And I bet that people on this call

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.420
were also doing research
that seemed quite relevant

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:02.300
at the moment.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:06.053
And then with this huge
disruption that happened,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.500
we felt like, at least in our group,

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.380
that we had to shift priorities

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:15.280
and maybe start doing some research,

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.030
or maybe just even getting together,

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:20.363
I think, was the initial initial thought,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.270
around understanding
what the impact would be

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:27.510
for people with mental health challenges

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.860
or people who were in
the mental health system.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:36.580
Two narratives emerged very
early on in the pandemic

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:40.233
about mental health.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:47.170
And part of this work
was to actually challenge

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:51.830
some of these conceptualizations
that emerged very early on.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:55.760
One of them is that the COVID-19 pandemic

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.080
would bring a tsunami of mental illness.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:04.080
This was something that the
media was talking about,

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.780
but it was something
that also made its way

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:11.190
into academic literature and research.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:16.190
And it looked pretty early
on that conceptualizations

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:21.410
of how people would respond
to a global emergence

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:25.040
of an unknown virus
that was killing people

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.970
by the thousands daily,

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:32.760
that that would be framed
as a mental challenge

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:34.503
or a mental illness.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:40.550
And that really kind
of got our antennas up

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:45.030
because there is a history in our field

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:49.580
of centering attention to the individual

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:53.220
rather than understanding
the social determinants

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.580
of why human suffering happens
in certain circumstances

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.783
and why it manifests the way it does.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:03.970
So this was kind of one
initial thing that we felt,

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:07.643
or our group felt like we
needed the challenge early on.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:12.810
And then the other response
to a tsunami of mental illness

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:16.980
is to ramp up services.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:19.900
So the idea that more
people would become ill

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:22.300
and more people would need services

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:27.110
would prompt a response in
increasing access to service,

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:30.660
increasing services offered.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.510
And we also wanted to challenge that

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:35.940
and include

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.720
a view more grounded and rooted

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.550
in social determinants of health

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:49.420
rather than individual pathology,
if I can put it that way.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.070
And that's not to say that
there isn't suffering,

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:56.700
but it's really, how do
we understand suffering

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.150
in such an extreme context,

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.500
and how do we draw the line

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:06.500
of what suffering is
appropriate, acceptable, normal,

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:12.050
and what suffering warrants
treatments, services, diagnoses.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:17.120
So really, what the
pandemic kind of re-centered

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:21.080
are some questions that
are part of psychiatry

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:24.723
and are part of the
so-called side disciplines,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.200
since their emergence as disciplines.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.040
And so, they're not really new problems,

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:37.040
but they were just kind
of centered very suddenly

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:42.670
once the world response

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:47.320
to the pandemic started
being conceptualized

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.433
as mental illness.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:55.300
And another element that pretty early on

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.570
also appeared in the narrative

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.920
is this idea of crisis as opportunity.

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.100
And for some curious reason,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.970
for something different entirely,

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:13.483
I was rereading Susan Sontag's,

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:17.090
one of the books that she wrote

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.157
that's called "Illness as Metaphor."

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:24.360
The book actually comes with two,

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:27.490
the book that I got is two books of hers.

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:29.370
And one is "Illness as Metaphor"

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:31.990
and then the other is
"AIDS and Its Metaphors."

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:35.563
And that's a book she wrote
about the AIDS epidemic.

00:13:37.396 --> 00:13:40.810
And I was just surprised to see

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:43.400
a type of prophetic

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:47.090
kind of content in this book.

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:51.790
And as I read it, I felt like
I could just switch words

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:55.200
and kind of see a complete repetition

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.040
of something that had happened in the past

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.463
with the AIDS epidemic,

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:03.030
including the inequities and inequalities

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:07.040
that were seen in the day,
back with the AIDS epidemic,

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:11.850
but also some of the
metaphors that it instilled.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:15.320
And it was just very interesting to,

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:20.320
also, she quotes the, at the time,

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:24.130
the head of the WHO
saying things very similar

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.640
to what we were hearing the
current head of the WHO say.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.440
So it was quite interesting

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:33.840
to see how history repeats itself

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:37.130
and how it's not always that we learned

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:39.490
something from history.

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:43.120
So in terms of the crisis as opportunity,

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.600
that's an interesting idea,

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.133
but we wanted to challenge
in the sense that,

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:54.210
opportunity for whom, and
who takes this opportunity?

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:57.850
And how is crisis being conceptualized?

00:14:57.850 --> 00:15:01.390
I think all of this, for instance,

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:04.160
for the global north,
this looked a certain way

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.070
with the pandemic, with the vaccines,

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.390
and with the ability to provide services.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.070
But then in the global south,

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:12.600
it certainly looked very different.

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.140
And for instance, one of our colleagues

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.860
who was based in Brazil was
doing some research around

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:21.060
how COVID was being experienced

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.840
in the favelas of Brazil.

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.810
And then for the people
that they were interviewing,

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.850
that wasn't a crisis at all,
it was just another thing.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:35.450
Crisis were related to
hunger, to not having food,

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:37.100
to not having a roof over your head,

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:40.290
and to problems of basic subsistence

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:44.630
that were really much more important

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:47.230
than another virus.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.920
And so, this idea of crisis is an idea

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:54.890
that has a place

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:58.770
and that has a context to exist.

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:02.300
And also, this idea of opportunity,

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.250
opportunity for whom and
what type of opportunity?

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.650
And also, did we take this opportunity,

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:10.803
something that was on our mind.

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:15.770
So today, I'll talk
about how we went about

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:18.623
addressing these issues.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:22.583
How do we engage and understand this?

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:28.730
The way we did it is just
by going to the source.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:32.220
I think that we, early
on, came up with a number

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.150
of strategies to engage with people

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:35.773
in multiple different ways,

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.700
both through ways that would
be considered research,

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:41.700
but also in other ways.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.453
We did a number of webinars and podcasts

00:16:45.453 --> 00:16:47.610
that I'll talk about,

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:49.910
we wrote a commentary and published it,

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.100
we did listening sessions.

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:54.340
So it was really a mix of
a lot of different ways

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:55.500
to engage with people.

00:16:55.500 --> 00:17:00.330
And we ended up, I think,
engaging with many more

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:03.330
than we would've expected
at the beginning.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:07.050
So what I'll do today in my
talk is talk a little bit

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.710
about those listening sessions,

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.040
what we learned there
and how that informed

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:16.203
the following stages of this project,

00:17:17.090 --> 00:17:17.990
what we published,

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:20.460
the podcast series that we did,

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.340
the webinars, and
finally the focus groups,

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:25.780
and those were the regional conversations

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:29.993
that were national in kind of geography,

00:17:32.888 --> 00:17:35.260
and kind of finishing up
with some final thoughts

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:38.230
that I hope won't be too
disappointing. (laughs)

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:40.210
And every time I see researchers talking,

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.010
I hear, you know, I'm good
at asking more questions

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:44.010
and never answering anything.

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:48.750
And I feel like I might be
blamed for the same problem,

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:52.210
but I'll do my best to keep us all engaged

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:54.390
in this conversation.

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:58.130
I should also add that there is a reason

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:02.553
why we wanted to discuss marginalization,

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:07.750
and mental health, and oppression.

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:12.280
So those issues, I mean,
as Priya was saying

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.660
at the beginning, if folks thought,

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:17.030
and I feel like I was naive enough

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:19.890
to think that this might
be the great equalizer

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:21.920
and that it would affect everyone.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.590
And so, maybe this
would bring us together.

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:26.930
I guess, really, early days, I was trying

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:31.423
to remain optimistic, but
then very, very early on,

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:37.670
we saw incredible disparities,
and that the pandemic

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:41.583
was really affecting people
in very, very different ways.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:47.220
And those different ways are not random.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:50.050
Those different ways are
related to the inequities

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:54.170
and the structural issues
that have been part of society

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:58.810
for a long, long time,
and have gone unaddressed,

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:03.810
and then came squarely to the
fore as the pandemic emerged.

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:08.430
And I'll just give a
brief, I don't wanna draw

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:13.460
too many statistics for
this talk as my research

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:14.700
is mostly qualitative,

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:18.150
and I like to be very close
to what people tell us,

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:20.170
and you'll see that there
will be a lot of quotes

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:21.210
as we move along.

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:25.270
But I'll just say that
for a person diagnosed

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:26.960
with a mental disorder,

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.730
the mortality of COVID was much higher

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:32.600
than persons without a diagnosis.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:35.630
And within this group of people

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:37.480
with the psychiatric diagnosis,

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:39.360
people diagnosed with schizophrenia

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:42.390
had an even higher mortality rate.

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:46.773
And that is considered a
serious mental illness.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:52.322
And then I'll also add that when compared

00:19:52.322 --> 00:19:55.310
to White Americans and adjusted for age,

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:58.383
African Americans are
3.6 times more likely.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:03.030
Native Americans are
3.4 times more likely.

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:07.993
And Latinx are 3.2 times more
likely to die from COVID-19.

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:13.343
So that really also
informed a lot of our work.

00:20:14.244 --> 00:20:18.600
And so, those were very sobering things

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:19.880
that we learned early on.

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:24.880
I wanna say that, I wish it surprised me,

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:27.460
but once we started learning

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.683
and once the research started coming out,

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.350
I have to say that anyone
that's in this area

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:39.350
knows that these inequities are
in place and it was expected

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:42.903
that they would affect people differently.

00:20:44.725 --> 00:20:47.770
So I wanted to start by presenting

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:49.823
the listening sessions that we had.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:53.370
So listening sessions are just a way

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:58.370
of convening people in a
slightly informal forum

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:00.770
without much of an agenda.

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:04.160
This was so early, this was April, 2021.

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:08.690
So this was very early on in the pandemic.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.670
And what we really wanted
to do was to understand

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:16.350
whether people had had
a previous experience

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:17.970
with the mental health system,

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:19.820
whether it be being hospitalized

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.840
because of a mental health issue,

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:26.943
or having had a diagnosis
of a mental disorder,

00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:32.300
how people were understanding the pandemic

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:35.570
and how were they, and this has a history,

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:39.310
it comes from an idea that also

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:43.160
was a narrative that early on emerges,

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:47.230
that people who have a previous diagnosis

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:48.800
of mental health challenges

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:55.170
would likely be more affected
or affected in a worse way,

00:21:55.820 --> 00:22:00.820
or have poor outcomes than
people without a diagnosis.

00:22:01.424 --> 00:22:04.210
So we just wanted to understand

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:06.870
a little bit how people were dealing

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:10.310
with having to isolate, and
having to shelter in place,

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:13.646
and just kind of,

00:22:13.646 --> 00:22:16.660
it was a very informal
set of conversations

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:21.580
to lay the groundwork for the
other engagements that we did.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:22.970
And so, a couple of themes

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:25.840
that I thought would be
interesting to share with you

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:30.840
is that, first of all,
so many experts emerged

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:34.640
as the pandemic hit.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:37.020
And one of the questions that we heard

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:41.370
in our listening sessions
were, "Who are the experts?"

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:43.640
So all this stuff about
surviving a pandemic,

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.340
and that's a direct
quote of a participant,

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:49.473
expert advice, as if anybody
alive has experienced

00:22:49.473 --> 00:22:52.320
a pandemic before and is an expert in it.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.420
So I think that was very interesting

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:57.450
because psychologists, psychiatrist,

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:58.760
and mental health professionals

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:02.480
were being called to
talk about the pandemic

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:06.660
and kind of the mental
health impact of it.

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:08.400
And it was just very interesting

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:09.860
because this is one instance

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:12.330
where we are all going
through it together.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:16.440
And I can't really say as a
clinical psychologist myself

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:19.160
that I have more experience than someone

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:24.100
that I'm seeing as a client
or a patient, than them.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:28.940
And so, in that sense, it kind
of levels the power dynamics

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:33.940
between professionals
and non-professionals.

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:37.030
So that was something
that came up early on.

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:41.160
And we also hoped that that would allow us

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:44.160
to think more critically
about these power imbalances

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:47.630
that are part of
therapeutic relationships,

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:51.180
and that the expert knows
more about you than yourself

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:53.927
and kind of flip that on
its head and think that,

00:23:53.927 --> 00:23:58.927
"Well, maybe the expert
on my own life is me

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.717
and not the person that I'm
seeing as a professional."

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:05.040
And that's something that's part

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:08.780
of kind of the critical work in this area,

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:10.367
the area of mental health.

00:24:10.367 --> 00:24:13.130
So there was some hope

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:15.480
that maybe we could talk
more about these issues

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:18.010
now that everyone would be going

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.200
through this pandemic together

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:23.290
and kind of experiencing
it at the same time,

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:27.290
and it'd be very hard
to claim being an expert

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:30.080
anymore than any other person.

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:32.580
So that was an interesting
thing we learned there.

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:35.870
And then also, some knowledge and wisdom

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:40.270
from direct experience with mental health,

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:42.980
with hospitalizations and with,

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:47.400
you know, maybe even
coercive mental health

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:48.873
kind of treatments.

00:24:50.624 --> 00:24:54.370
And then I think one thing
we heard a number of times

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:56.300
is that people have lived experience,

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:57.830
have lived experience in trauma,

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:00.880
but they also have lived
experience in recovery.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:03.270
And so, in some ways, in
these listening sessions,

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:07.890
we heard a lot that some of us that,

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:09.570
you know, a previous history,

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.710
with a mental health challenge,

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:14.780
could be seen as putting people

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:16.880
in a more vulnerable position,

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:18.390
was actually being conceptualized

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:20.200
by the people that we were listening to

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:23.580
by actually having had some experience

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.343
that is useful for this context,

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:30.860
and being sometimes more
prepared than their counterparts

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:35.520
who never had any lived
experience with trauma,

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:36.850
or direct experience with trauma,

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:38.983
or a psychiatric diagnosis.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:43.940
So that was an interesting
take on things as well.

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:47.120
Maybe what was thought as a vulnerability

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:49.823
is actually kind of an asset.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:52.530
Something else that we heard

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:56.180
that was interesting and
not surprising to me,

00:25:56.180 --> 00:25:58.090
but it might be surprising to some,

00:25:58.090 --> 00:26:01.610
is a lot of people said,
"Well, welcome to my life."

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:03.800
Some people have to self isolate anyway

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.930
because of chronic illness
or other conditions

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:07.690
that make it harder for them to get out.

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:09.640
And they're like,
"Welcome to my experiences

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:11.257
on a regular basis."

00:26:12.588 --> 00:26:13.880
And we heard different things as well.

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:15.110
So we heard from people

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:17.960
who were hospitalized multiple times,

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:22.080
for instance, that they
were told what to do before.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.480
This is not the first time
that they're being told

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.903
that they can't leave the
room, that they can't leave,

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:31.000
or that they can't do something
that they wanted to do.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:34.400
And so, not to romanticize
those experiences

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:36.270
or say that it's good
to go through trauma,

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:37.210
'cause that protects you

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:41.190
from even more traumatic
experiences in the future,

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:43.940
but just to say that
some of the assumptions

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:47.890
about fragility and about vulnerability,

00:26:47.890 --> 00:26:51.250
related to how people go
through these experiences,

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:55.893
may not map out to their real experiences.

00:26:57.030 --> 00:27:01.420
And one huge concern that
came up time and time again

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:07.150
was, I think, kind of the
driver of this whole project,

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:12.150
is I wonder how we can
counter that narrative

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.200
and not have this be an opportunity

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:17.570
to funnel more people into
the mental health system.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:20.050
I worry that that's where we'll go

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:24.430
instead of social change and
addressing disparities issues.

00:27:24.430 --> 00:27:27.860
And I think this direct quote
from one of our participants

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:32.320
really was kind of decisive

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:36.750
for taking the route that we did.

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:39.110
And so, those listening sessions

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:41.580
really shaped all the other engagements

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:46.440
that we planned for this project,

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:48.110
beyond the research piece.

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:49.923
But you'll see that we also had,

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:53.380
the webinars and the podcasts

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.440
were more related to engaging with people

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:57.793
rather than to extract data.

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:03.290
And then I'll show us a quote

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:07.420
that really was one of the
most striking for me early on

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:10.323
and made me, it was really provocative.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:15.017
So someone in our listening session said,

00:28:15.017 --> 00:28:16.470
"When I went psychotic,

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:19.820
people talked about me
personally breaking from reality.

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:22.900
But it seems like reality
is breaking from all of us.

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:25.030
So what do you call that?"

00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:30.030
And that was a very, to
me, important provocation

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:34.820
in terms of how we
conceptualize mental illness,

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:37.450
mental health challenges, mental problems,

00:28:37.450 --> 00:28:38.893
however you wanna call it.

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:44.080
What happens when something so disruptive

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:46.320
happens in reality,

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:51.320
in shared and consensus
reality, what do we call it?

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:55.090
And then from those listening sessions,

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:57.860
we actually wrote a
commentary which we published.

00:28:57.860 --> 00:29:00.760
And the commentary was coauthored

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:04.990
by a number of people
who are not academics,

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:08.703
who are people who have
had a previous diagnosis,

00:29:08.703 --> 00:29:11.503
a mental health diagnosis,
or people who were activists.

00:29:12.367 --> 00:29:17.367
So this is one example of
kind of how it's possible

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:22.450
to maybe bridge some of these gaps

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:25.040
between academia and activism,

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:29.853
or academia and kind of
regular life, I would say,

00:29:31.045 --> 00:29:34.020
in that we really invited everyone

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:37.110
who participated in the listening
sessions to be co-authors.

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:40.693
And it was really written
by multiple hands.

00:29:42.136 --> 00:29:44.117
And so, we published that early on.

00:29:44.117 --> 00:29:45.870
"When reality breaks from us:

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:49.840
lived experience wisdom
in the COVID-19 era."

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:52.997
And we did outline some of those themes

00:29:52.997 --> 00:29:56.773
and kind of thought about some
directions for the future.

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:03.550
And then the other piece that we did,

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:07.080
I mean, we just didn't
wanna do research only.

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:10.910
We wanted to take the
interdisciplinary aspect of things

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:15.750
to another level, in that we
not only wanted to do research

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:17.550
between different disciplines,

00:30:17.550 --> 00:30:20.260
but we also wanted to
engage different media,

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:25.260
and engage people that are
sitting in different places,

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:28.460
including some policy makers.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:30.180
And some of the names
that you'll see there

00:30:30.180 --> 00:30:32.610
are policy makers and some people

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:35.130
who are working within the system,

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:39.130
and then a mix with people
who have lived experience

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:40.970
or direct experience.

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:43.750
And so, we did this podcast series

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:48.430
in which we just talked
rather quite informally

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:50.913
about the issues that were coming up.

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:55.610
So that was in partnership
with C4 Innovations

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:58.950
and the Mental Health
Technology Transfer Center

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:01.360
for the New England region.

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:04.700
And we covered a lot of topics,

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:07.800
from surviving the mental health system,

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:10.850
to alternative understandings
of human suffering,

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:15.850
to peer support work, to social
justice and racial equity.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:21.130
And so, we kind of came up
with the themes together

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:24.440
based on what we were hearing
in our listening sessions,

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:28.930
but also what our core
team was kind of indicating

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:33.540
would be an interesting direction to go

00:31:33.540 --> 00:31:34.960
and the types of resources

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:37.160
that we thought people might want

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:39.363
but might not have access to.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:46.210
And then we also did this webinar series

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.493
that was also kind of, early on.

00:31:50.470 --> 00:31:52.150
And we did three separate webinars

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:53.270
that was also in partnership

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:55.580
with the Mental Health
Technology Transfer Center.

00:31:55.580 --> 00:31:58.210
And that reached a national audience

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:01.530
and folks from Canada as well.

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:06.320
We had a number of states
represented in these calls.

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:07.920
And they're available for anyone

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:10.493
who just wanna go into the website.

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:15.850
And so, we did one based
on that initial theme,

00:32:15.850 --> 00:32:17.940
welcome to my world.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:21.560
Then we did one on social
justice and COVID-19.

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:25.253
And we also did one on peer
support and essential workers.

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:30.730
I just wanna check how I'm doing on time.

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:31.563
Okay.

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:34.940
And now I just wanna transition

00:32:34.940 --> 00:32:39.380
to kinda the more researchy
phase of this project.

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:43.320
It's just a mix of different
methods and different things.

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:44.640
And we really wanted to make sure

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:49.210
that we were kind of weaving in research,

00:32:49.210 --> 00:32:53.040
and activism, and engaging
and connecting with people,

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:56.430
and not just doing research.

00:32:56.430 --> 00:33:00.300
So sometimes, I look at this and I wonder,

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:03.360
is this a little slightly
disjointed methodology?

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.610
But in fact, it was really the result

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:09.960
of us trying to figure out
how to navigate the space

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:12.010
and how to kind of rapidly respond

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:14.470
to the needs that we were seeing

00:33:16.520 --> 00:33:17.870
just in our daily lives.

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.250
It just felt like everyone
was going through everything

00:33:20.250 --> 00:33:23.920
at the same time and you
don't have the luxury

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:27.540
of hindsight or of time.

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:30.820
So with that kind of preliminary work

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:35.550
and those engagements
that we had early on,

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:40.333
then we went ahead and had
a number of focus groups.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:44.550
And that was a very long process

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:47.380
for our team to kind of develop

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:50.420
what we wanted to ask people,

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:54.080
but also how we wanted to capture

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:57.830
vulnerability, marginalization,

00:33:57.830 --> 00:34:00.933
and the experiences that we
were looking to understand.

00:34:02.660 --> 00:34:06.460
And so, we, through an iterative process

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:09.400
of going over the themes that we saw

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:10.640
in the listening sessions,

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:13.580
but also, being current
with the literature

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:15.693
that was coming out on this subject,

00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:20.890
we developed some areas of focus

00:34:20.890 --> 00:34:22.940
that we thought would be really important

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:27.430
to touch on.

00:34:27.430 --> 00:34:32.397
And so, we ended up
landing on these themes.

00:34:33.870 --> 00:34:37.370
And then when we recruited,
people were invited

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:41.400
to self-select to the group that they felt

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:44.250
was closer to their experience.

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:46.990
And so, for that, we had experiences

00:34:46.990 --> 00:34:49.730
with mental health and substance
use during the pandemic.

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:51.640
And we did actually two focus groups

00:34:51.640 --> 00:34:54.713
with that subject or that topic.

00:34:55.720 --> 00:34:58.080
We had experiences of poverty,

00:34:58.080 --> 00:35:02.810
and then people self-selected
to that group as well.

00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:06.650
Experiences with invisibility
and virus exposure,

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:11.280
and that was related to essential workers,

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.750
people who were not able
to shelter in place,

00:35:14.750 --> 00:35:18.350
such as bus drivers or
people in essential services,

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:21.083
like janitorial services in hospitals.

00:35:23.186 --> 00:35:24.530
And there was a lot of attention

00:35:24.530 --> 00:35:27.570
to how much healthcare professionals

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:29.670
were exposed to the virus,

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:32.860
but there was an entire
contingent of people

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:35.540
who were working daily,

00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:37.660
never sheltered in place and made sure

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:39.253
that services continued,

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:45.330
and that we're not clapped to,

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:48.640
and applauded, and thanked by society,

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:51.150
and in fact, we're getting
sick, and we're dying,

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:55.933
and we're in this kind
of invisibility position.

00:35:57.560 --> 00:36:00.700
And so, we wanted to make
sure that we talked to people

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.253
who had that experience.

00:36:03.710 --> 00:36:07.373
And then experiences with
housing and food insecurity.

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:12.170
We also wanted to know
how people experienced

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:15.000
kind of the rapid
emergence of some benefits

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:18.540
that were maybe long due,

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.980
but suddenly, when the pandemic started,

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:23.930
there was the eviction moratorium,

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:26.190
there was a number of different benefits

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:28.420
that people could access,
there were hotel rooms

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:31.970
for people who were
experiencing homelessness.

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:33.750
And so, it just seemed
like a lot of things

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:37.450
that people were advocating for

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:41.650
for a very long time
suddenly became possible.

00:36:41.650 --> 00:36:43.600
And we really wanted to understand

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:47.310
how people who have had these experiences

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:50.700
in the past were dealing with it now,

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:52.900
and the impact of these new programs

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:54.603
that emerged after COVID.

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:59.100
We also understood very clearly

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:01.460
that incarcerated populations

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:05.750
were being kind of left
behind in many ways

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:07.993
with COVID.

00:37:07.993 --> 00:37:09.350
And so, we wanted to make sure

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:12.970
that we also centered those experiences.

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:14.980
And we also wanted to talk to activists

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:17.670
and understand what happened to activism.

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:22.010
And did everything shift focus
when the pandemic started?

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:26.210
Was activism galvanized
by this or did it die out?

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:28.450
So we were really interested

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:30.823
in kind of hearing more
about that as well.

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:35.860
So, as I mentioned, it was a joint process

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:37.640
of developing these themes

00:37:38.919 --> 00:37:43.450
to make sure that we captured
a wide gamut of experiences

00:37:43.450 --> 00:37:46.100
of vulnerability,
marginalization, and oppression

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:48.783
that people could be experiencing.

00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:52.580
So the way we worked to disseminate this

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:55.560
and to recruit folks was we used a number

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:58.620
of activism-related listservs

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:00.510
and just our personal kind of connections,

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:04.350
but also, yeah, I mean,
just any type of listserv.

00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:07.210
It was really, we cast a wide, wide net.

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:09.010
And we really ended up getting folks

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:11.490
from all over the country participating

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.203
in these conversations.

00:38:15.430 --> 00:38:17.820
So we ended up, for this stage,

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:22.783
having 36 participants who
joined seven focus groups.

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:27.150
So that's just some of the characteristics

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:28.550
of people who participated.

00:38:28.550 --> 00:38:30.950
The mean age was 48-years-old.

00:38:30.950 --> 00:38:35.950
For those who did answer
the demographics questions,

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:41.340
we had 19 female, 3 male, and
1 fluid non-binary person.

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:45.210
Six participants identified
as Black or African American.

00:38:45.210 --> 00:38:48.330
1 identified as biracial and 14 as White.

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:50.480
And then a number of
people didn't fill out

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:52.260
the demographics,

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:55.140
so we don't have very
complete date on that.

00:38:55.140 --> 00:38:59.230
16 reported having one or
more psychiatric diagnoses

00:38:59.230 --> 00:39:01.683
and 8 reported substance use challenge.

00:39:02.690 --> 00:39:06.153
Nine reported having one
or more disabilities.

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:09.170
Eight of the participants reported

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:13.400
being on SSI or SSDI or are
receiving other benefits

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:16.220
to report a history of homelessness.

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:18.820
Five participants had a
history of incarceration

00:39:20.031 --> 00:39:22.833
or of a family member who
had been incarcerated.

00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:27.470
Most participants reported multiple

00:39:27.470 --> 00:39:29.673
and intersecting vulnerabilities.

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:33.840
And six participants had COVID themselves.

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:37.393
And another three had close
family members who had COVID.

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:44.130
So the first thing that
was kind of surprising,

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:46.120
I guess it shouldn't be, but it was,

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:48.590
because a lot of the research

00:39:48.590 --> 00:39:50.210
and a lot of the media narratives

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:53.610
sometimes makes us think
that things are linear

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:57.160
and kind of homogenous.

00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:01.020
But in fact, when we
started talking to people,

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:05.080
a lot of people described
the whole experience

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:09.650
of the pandemic in a positive light,

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:11.530
and found silver linings

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:16.530
or found very positive
things that happened

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:19.253
as a result of the pandemic.

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:24.350
But most people certainly
described challenges,

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:26.430
and that's obviously also by virtue

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:28.630
of what we wanted to talk about.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:31.087
But one of the positive things
that people talked about

00:40:31.087 --> 00:40:34.210
was being able to reconnect with family

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:37.383
around the world via Zoom,
and so feeling more connected.

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:40.297
Some people really described it as,

00:40:40.297 --> 00:40:41.723
"It was a nightmare.

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:45.960
I recycled to supplement my
food and my many other needs,

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:47.740
and I couldn't do it for the first month,

00:40:47.740 --> 00:40:49.650
we were all locked down.

00:40:49.650 --> 00:40:52.300
And then afterwards, still,
to this date, not safe.

00:40:52.300 --> 00:40:56.140
I don't feel safe, safe doing anymore."

00:40:56.140 --> 00:40:59.300
And then some people
described their experiences

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:00.360
just neutral.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:02.290
So "I think it wasn't
that disruptive for me

00:41:02.290 --> 00:41:06.300
simply because I'm older
and I'm more set in my ways.

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:08.520
I have the same routine
I've had for years.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.850
Plus, I'm able to entertain
myself because I have a lot

00:41:10.850 --> 00:41:13.720
of electronic entertainment in my home."

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:15.030
And so, we heard mixed things.

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:18.930
Like we kind of went in
maybe with our assumptions,

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:22.270
that it would be a really
challenging experience

00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:25.110
for everyone, but yeah, we learned

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:28.296
that there was just
this wide heterogeneity

00:41:28.296 --> 00:41:30.370
of how people experienced it.

00:41:30.370 --> 00:41:32.050
But not just how they experienced,

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:33.563
but how they talked about it.

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:39.250
In terms of isolation and connectedness,

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:41.970
I feel like that was a
theme that came up a lot.

00:41:41.970 --> 00:41:44.880
Most participants really
emphasized isolation,

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:47.553
loneliness, and lack of connection.

00:41:49.188 --> 00:41:50.021
So one example here,

00:41:50.021 --> 00:41:52.210
"I was so disconnected, so alone,

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:54.830
and in the psychotic state,
that I really needed people.

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:57.740
I was just too isolated."

00:41:57.740 --> 00:41:59.980
So some people did describe that.

00:41:59.980 --> 00:42:00.940
And then

00:42:02.780 --> 00:42:03.613
what else here?

00:42:03.613 --> 00:42:04.770
So "What we saw was that people

00:42:04.770 --> 00:42:07.580
not only were using the
previous connected channels

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:09.080
through their local clubhouses,

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:11.070
but were interacting beyond the scope

00:42:11.070 --> 00:42:14.047
of their small community."

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:17.140
So that's one example
of connectedness, right?

00:42:17.140 --> 00:42:19.240
So this is in the context of having

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:21.870
like 12 step groups online,

00:42:21.870 --> 00:42:26.730
and then some therapeutic
groups and meetings

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:29.470
that went beyond their communities,

00:42:29.470 --> 00:42:32.410
and actually reached many more people

00:42:32.410 --> 00:42:34.033
than they would've imagined.

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:39.750
In terms of the stimulus
checks and other benefits,

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:42.780
most participants who received them

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:44.590
found them to be very helpful.

00:42:44.590 --> 00:42:48.200
And some even experienced
financial and housing stability

00:42:48.200 --> 00:42:51.243
for the first time in very many years.

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:55.370
So a participant here, "I've
lived in a cycle of poverty

00:42:55.370 --> 00:42:57.330
almost my entire life.

00:42:57.330 --> 00:42:59.270
And basically, when I
was raising my daughter,

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:01.290
it was the only time I was really stable

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:03.490
as far as financially goes.

00:43:03.490 --> 00:43:07.620
And then just recently, with
a stimulus and unemployment,

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:08.967
very helpful."

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:15.270
We also heard a lot, and I think that

00:43:15.270 --> 00:43:18.900
that experience certainly transcends

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:23.590
any, I guess, any vulnerability
or marginalization,

00:43:23.590 --> 00:43:26.820
but the time's feeling
very politically fraught,

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:31.440
and participants talked a
lot about polarized views

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:33.700
and difficulties navigating

00:43:33.700 --> 00:43:37.000
very politically-charged conversations.

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:42.000
And then one example, I guess, is,

00:43:42.290 --> 00:43:43.433
we can see here,

00:43:44.507 --> 00:43:46.760
"I would love to get
another stimulus check,

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:48.630
but I don't think we deserve it.

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:50.280
I saw too many people using it

00:43:50.280 --> 00:43:52.690
for reasons that they
shouldn't have used it for.

00:43:52.690 --> 00:43:55.427
So I don't think we deserve
another stimulus check."

00:43:57.570 --> 00:44:02.410
So some of this was in very
kind of charged context

00:44:02.410 --> 00:44:05.050
and with very strong views.

00:44:05.050 --> 00:44:07.850
And then others here,
"I've stopped talking

00:44:07.850 --> 00:44:09.980
about COVID-19 to people

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:12.950
because it's become so
politicized in my family.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:16.980
I'm not gonna get a lot into them."

00:44:16.980 --> 00:44:19.303
And so, this feeling was really,

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:24.650
it came up a lot and
with very strong views

00:44:24.650 --> 00:44:27.253
about various different issues.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:31.090
In terms of activism in people

00:44:31.090 --> 00:44:35.213
that have been activists before,

00:44:36.620 --> 00:44:40.560
they had really described
this kind of an awareness

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:42.960
of the need to continue activism,

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:44.880
but also a sense of urgency

00:44:44.880 --> 00:44:47.513
that may have not been there before.

00:44:48.820 --> 00:44:49.817
And so, one example here,

00:44:49.817 --> 00:44:53.390
"I'm not sure that I increased
my activism or decreased.

00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:55.530
It definitely didn't
decrease during the pandemic,

00:44:55.530 --> 00:44:58.607
but it definitely became a
bit more urgent, I would say."

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:02.117
And then another participant here,

00:45:02.117 --> 00:45:04.400
"I figured out a way to
shake the foundation.

00:45:04.400 --> 00:45:05.830
I got active.

00:45:05.830 --> 00:45:08.260
It wasn't that I really
wanted to become active,

00:45:08.260 --> 00:45:11.250
but I probably should have been
doing this a long time ago.

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:13.110
But it stood up my activism

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:15.370
of how I could change
the outcomes of someone

00:45:15.370 --> 00:45:18.360
in certain circumstances
that I was witnessing."

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:21.830
So we did hear a lot of
that in the focus groups,

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:26.163
that people just felt activated.

00:45:30.570 --> 00:45:32.300
And then in terms of poverty,

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:35.700
financial challenges
associated with the pandemic

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:39.880
and the benefits that people received,

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:42.883
those were discussed quite extensively.

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:48.390
And then I would just read
the participants' quotes.

00:45:49.597 --> 00:45:52.680
"Financially, I work one full-time job

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:56.050
and two part-time jobs,
all at the same place.

00:45:56.050 --> 00:45:58.010
I lost the two part-time jobs.

00:45:58.010 --> 00:46:01.920
So it was a struggle to pay
my bills, but I'm making it.

00:46:01.920 --> 00:46:06.160
It's hard and I still have
anxiety attack from it."

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:08.350
Someone else say, "Oh,
health wise, you know,

00:46:08.350 --> 00:46:09.500
I'm not working anymore.

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:11.810
I went on SSDI my sister said,

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:15.197
you know, financially, I took a big hit."

00:46:16.490 --> 00:46:18.330
Someone else said, "I've lost money.

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:22.520
There's monetary things thing
in terms of being affected.

00:46:22.520 --> 00:46:24.710
I had just had surgery, but during COVID,

00:46:24.710 --> 00:46:26.200
I've been a part-time musician.

00:46:26.200 --> 00:46:29.390
I couldn't do any gigs so I lost money

00:46:29.390 --> 00:46:31.157
that's a lot of money."

00:46:33.600 --> 00:46:35.390
And then someone, "But then I was eligible

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:36.890
for the pandemic unemployment.

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:38.420
And that was really helpful.

00:46:38.420 --> 00:46:39.620
I started building my credit,

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:42.470
returned to college to
study cybersecurity.

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:43.690
And that's good.

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:45.813
And paid off my driver's license fines,

00:46:46.834 --> 00:46:49.340
was able to send my brother
who was incarcerated

00:46:49.340 --> 00:46:52.590
some money, and was able to
get my daughter some money."

00:46:52.590 --> 00:46:57.240
So really overwhelmingly
positive experiences

00:46:57.240 --> 00:46:59.693
with receiving additional benefits.

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:06.723
And then in terms of injustices
that people talked about,

00:47:08.540 --> 00:47:12.200
some people said, "I've spent a lot of it,

00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:15.900
a lot of the pandemic being
very angry with a lot of people,

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:18.090
including government officials.

00:47:18.090 --> 00:47:19.620
I'm trying to work my way through that

00:47:19.620 --> 00:47:21.870
and in realizing that
some policy decisions

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:25.360
that they made that seemed
profoundly inequitable,

00:47:25.360 --> 00:47:29.000
especially for people with
disabilities at the time."

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:32.920
And then just, "Everyone
should be treated equally

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:34.513
because we're all human beings.

00:47:36.294 --> 00:47:39.190
Where you come from, if
you're Black or White,

00:47:39.190 --> 00:47:41.510
rich or poor, you should
be treated with dignity

00:47:41.510 --> 00:47:42.757
when you are sick."

00:47:46.470 --> 00:47:50.070
And then we also had, you
know, racism was a topic

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:54.380
that came up in most of
the groups that we did,

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:56.980
and then in various different ways,

00:47:56.980 --> 00:47:59.173
which shouldn't be surprising either.

00:48:00.979 --> 00:48:05.630
So one quote here, "And here
is one way I think everyone

00:48:05.630 --> 00:48:08.800
here has agreed that clearly,
we got a racist system here.

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:09.730
That's deadly.

00:48:09.730 --> 00:48:10.807
We got that."

00:48:11.720 --> 00:48:14.697
And then someone talking
about their own experience.

00:48:14.697 --> 00:48:17.040
"It took me back to
when I was 11 years old,

00:48:17.040 --> 00:48:18.560
when I was stopped by the police

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:20.680
and they frisked me and they checked me.

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:21.900
And they said I looked like someone

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:23.610
who had committed a crime.

00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:26.700
At 11-years-old, I knew
that I could be vulnerable

00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:28.430
for certain things.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:31.920
COVID has let me know,
has let me also know

00:48:31.920 --> 00:48:34.320
about the disparities in healthcare.

00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:36.490
Poor people, Black
people, and brown people

00:48:36.490 --> 00:48:38.307
have been such high numbers."

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:43.920
And then one, I was kind of divided

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:46.560
of whether I should
keep this quote or not,

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:50.223
but I decided to keep it here and read it.

00:48:52.700 --> 00:48:55.460
I certainly had never, and
I've been in the United States

00:48:55.460 --> 00:48:56.570
for three years now.

00:48:56.570 --> 00:48:58.720
As Priya mentioned, I'm
originally from Brazil.

00:48:58.720 --> 00:49:01.430
And I don't think I had ever encountered

00:49:01.430 --> 00:49:04.313
something as direct as this before.

00:49:05.560 --> 00:49:08.920
And that maybe, by virtue
of being an academic center,

00:49:08.920 --> 00:49:13.780
but I should read this quote and say that,

00:49:13.780 --> 00:49:16.917
you know, one of our
participants said very clearly,

00:49:16.917 --> 00:49:19.480
"But I tell people I am racist.

00:49:19.480 --> 00:49:22.000
And I will be racist till the day I die.

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:23.430
I grew up in a racist family.

00:49:23.430 --> 00:49:25.170
Seeing a Black man walking down the street

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:26.500
and calling him up.

00:49:26.500 --> 00:49:30.410
N word was nothing to me in St.
Paul, when I was growing up.

00:49:30.410 --> 00:49:32.450
But to me, I can be friends with someone

00:49:32.450 --> 00:49:33.600
who's Native American.

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:36.100
I can be friends with someone
who's African American.

00:49:36.100 --> 00:49:38.360
But I don't like the organization.

00:49:38.360 --> 00:49:39.810
I don't like Black Lives Matter.

00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:41.520
I don't like the other stuff.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:44.170
But I have friends who are
all different races and stuff.

00:49:44.170 --> 00:49:47.677
So, yeah, it's kind of a
catch 22, if I can say that."

00:49:48.560 --> 00:49:53.560
So it was really a very broad,

00:49:53.676 --> 00:49:58.140
I guess this was one
participant out of the 36

00:49:58.140 --> 00:49:59.150
that we interviewed.

00:49:59.150 --> 00:50:01.040
And the majority of views aligned

00:50:01.040 --> 00:50:05.760
with the views that you see
on the previous two quotes.

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:09.560
But that's just an example, I guess,

00:50:09.560 --> 00:50:13.000
of how much work we still have to do

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:16.513
around racial equity,
about anti-racism work.

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:21.290
Because I feel like people,

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:23.350
some people might still
be very comfortable

00:50:23.350 --> 00:50:26.293
in saying what this
participant said, for instance.

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:30.660
And I also wanted to bring some attention

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:34.980
to what limitations we had in
these groups that we convened.

00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:37.910
I think one of them is that participants

00:50:37.910 --> 00:50:40.358
really learned about this study,

00:50:40.358 --> 00:50:43.890
a lot of them, via activism networks.

00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:47.850
And so, we could expect
that these could be people

00:50:47.850 --> 00:50:49.760
who are just more likely to be engaged

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:54.760
or who have less challenges
with technology, for instance.

00:50:57.040 --> 00:50:59.810
So we might have missed
a lot of the people

00:50:59.810 --> 00:51:02.730
that we did wanna engage just by virtue

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:04.313
of how we recruited folks.

00:51:09.605 --> 00:51:11.950
And so, some final thoughts that I wanna,

00:51:11.950 --> 00:51:14.110
and this is my last slide,

00:51:14.110 --> 00:51:16.530
and I'm moving towards the end of my talk,

00:51:16.530 --> 00:51:18.400
and would be really interested

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:21.120
in engaging in conversation

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:24.900
with everyone that's in the room,

00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:29.390
but one of the, I think, key things

00:51:29.390 --> 00:51:32.033
that we as researchers,

00:51:33.630 --> 00:51:36.170
and as policy makers, and as clinicians,

00:51:36.170 --> 00:51:38.140
and wherever we're sitting,

00:51:38.140 --> 00:51:40.340
I think we need to
think a little bit about

00:51:41.790 --> 00:51:44.740
how a lot of the mental health discourse

00:51:44.740 --> 00:51:49.740
is sometimes obliterating
some really important issues

00:51:50.380 --> 00:51:52.420
related to poverty,

00:51:52.420 --> 00:51:56.700
related to social determinants of health,

00:51:56.700 --> 00:52:01.260
and related to issues that are
more systemic in our society

00:52:01.260 --> 00:52:03.453
rather than at the individual level.

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:08.190
I think one challenge is
how to actually connect

00:52:08.190 --> 00:52:11.350
those various levels, both conceptually,

00:52:11.350 --> 00:52:14.120
so that our practices can be more guided

00:52:15.330 --> 00:52:18.500
towards approaches that are informed

00:52:20.240 --> 00:52:22.463
by these multiple determinants,

00:52:23.980 --> 00:52:28.030
but also in terms of
what type of research,

00:52:28.030 --> 00:52:29.880
where money is being invested,

00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:32.593
where funding goes for
these types of things,

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:35.590
if we wanna ramp up services,

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:38.160
or do we want to expand

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:42.500
a social net so that people don't fall

00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:43.750
through the cracks so much,

00:52:43.750 --> 00:52:47.180
and that people actually
have an opportunity

00:52:47.180 --> 00:52:49.550
to climb out of the poverty cycle

00:52:49.550 --> 00:52:52.403
that a lot of participants talked about.

00:52:55.890 --> 00:52:58.070
So this was conceptualized
by some authors,

00:52:58.070 --> 00:53:01.330
by Helen Henson, for instance,
as pathologizing poverty.

00:53:01.330 --> 00:53:04.810
But Joanna Moncrieff,
who's another author,

00:53:04.810 --> 00:53:09.120
also conceptualized it
as medicalizing misery.

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:13.820
And a lot of it has happened
after welfare reform

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:18.093
in the United States, after 1996,

00:53:19.210 --> 00:53:21.640
in that really one of the few pathways

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:26.170
towards more stable benefits
currently in the United States

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:29.423
is through a psychiatric
diagnosis or chronic pain.

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:32.963
And that has impacts.

00:53:33.921 --> 00:53:36.170
And Helen's work shows that way beyond

00:53:37.100 --> 00:53:40.350
having a disability,
but also the subjective

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:43.550
and impacts of having to

00:53:44.580 --> 00:53:48.820
identify yourself as
someone who's disabled,

00:53:48.820 --> 00:53:51.520
and also the stigma that
that brings for people

00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:55.180
actually end up institutionalizing

00:53:55.180 --> 00:53:58.733
ways of being in the world
and in forms of existence.

00:53:59.690 --> 00:54:04.690
So it has the potential to create harm

00:54:05.250 --> 00:54:07.670
way beyond some of the more directly

00:54:07.670 --> 00:54:09.340
and easily anticipated

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:17.090
results of a kind of a welfare system

00:54:17.090 --> 00:54:18.800
that is structured in this way,

00:54:18.800 --> 00:54:21.330
that a lot of kind of the gate-keeping

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:26.000
happens through the medical system.

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:30.350
And I think this idea of
crisis as a golden opportunity,

00:54:30.350 --> 00:54:33.780
that's also, I mean, that was
kind of just a direct quote

00:54:33.780 --> 00:54:36.480
from one of the papers
that came out early on

00:54:36.480 --> 00:54:38.010
in the pandemic.

00:54:38.010 --> 00:54:40.290
I mean, it really makes you wonder.

00:54:40.290 --> 00:54:42.510
I mean, the pandemic hasn't really been

00:54:42.510 --> 00:54:44.080
a great equalizer at all.

00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:48.560
If anything, it just
reveals even more clearly

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:53.410
the horrendous inequities,

00:54:53.410 --> 00:54:55.173
both in health,

00:54:56.930 --> 00:55:01.090
and the systemic racism is
something that came to the fore

00:55:02.510 --> 00:55:06.593
very acutely in this time,
the oppression, poverty.

00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:09.630
You know, lack of universal healthcare

00:55:09.630 --> 00:55:12.560
in the richest country of the world

00:55:12.560 --> 00:55:15.150
is something that, you
know, as a discussion

00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:18.473
that I didn't see a lot of.

00:55:19.980 --> 00:55:23.930
And so, was it really a golden opportunity

00:55:23.930 --> 00:55:25.060
or have we missed it?

00:55:25.060 --> 00:55:26.180
Is there still time?

00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:29.093
Those are questions that
I continue to ask myself.

00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:33.830
It really certainly was
not the great equalizer.

00:55:33.830 --> 00:55:35.770
I don't know what we're gonna start seeing

00:55:35.770 --> 00:55:39.890
now that the eviction
moratorium was lifted

00:55:39.890 --> 00:55:44.010
and what the consequences
will be of rolling back

00:55:44.010 --> 00:55:46.920
some of the supports
that were put in place

00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:49.230
when the pandemic started,

00:55:49.230 --> 00:55:52.830
and what are gonna be the consequences

00:55:52.830 --> 00:55:56.630
of the economic crisis

00:55:56.630 --> 00:55:59.310
coupled with a sudden loss

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:04.090
of the few supports that
people had access to

00:56:04.090 --> 00:56:05.463
just to stay afloat.

00:56:06.835 --> 00:56:11.640
And really, so I think that
kind of the takeaway message

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:13.210
here for us was that, you know,

00:56:13.210 --> 00:56:17.500
there's a need to articulate
these equally important levels

00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:18.603
of the issue.

00:56:19.470 --> 00:56:22.770
So the direct experience of the individual

00:56:22.770 --> 00:56:25.510
and how people were
suffering or struggling

00:56:25.510 --> 00:56:28.730
is an incredibly important element,

00:56:28.730 --> 00:56:31.910
but how are they weaved in and connected

00:56:31.910 --> 00:56:36.590
with the more systemic
and societal levels,

00:56:36.590 --> 00:56:39.790
and how can we actually
address those things

00:56:39.790 --> 00:56:42.153
beyond just the individual.

00:56:45.180 --> 00:56:48.970
And then I also think that
one of the successful elements

00:56:48.970 --> 00:56:52.930
of this work was that it
really centered the voices

00:56:52.930 --> 00:56:57.380
of people who had firsthand experiences

00:56:57.380 --> 00:57:00.780
in every stage of research,

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:04.830
from planning, to executing,

00:57:04.830 --> 00:57:09.020
to analyzing the data, and
to planning next steps.

00:57:09.020 --> 00:57:12.210
And so, I continue to advocate

00:57:12.210 --> 00:57:15.010
and be a firm believer that
we can only find solutions

00:57:15.010 --> 00:57:17.290
for these complicated issues

00:57:17.290 --> 00:57:21.200
if we center the voices
and the experiences

00:57:21.200 --> 00:57:24.110
of those who are most affected by it.

00:57:24.110 --> 00:57:28.740
And so, I think this is
a good time to wrap up.

00:57:28.740 --> 00:57:32.040
I, again, am so thankful
for this invitation,

00:57:32.040 --> 00:57:36.450
and I hope that we can
have a discussion together

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:38.480
and a conversation.

00:57:38.480 --> 00:57:41.800
So thank you very much,
again, to Priya and Ty

00:57:41.800 --> 00:57:43.150
for putting this together,

00:57:43.150 --> 00:57:45.713
and to the Frankes for
supporting this work.

00:57:47.370 --> 00:57:50.200
- Thank you so much, Anna, for sharing

00:57:50.200 --> 00:57:53.933
these wonderful insights
from this project.

00:57:54.950 --> 00:57:57.420
If I may, there is a question in the chat,

00:57:57.420 --> 00:58:02.290
but if I may open up by asking
the first question myself.

00:58:02.290 --> 00:58:06.360
I was curious about, you
mentioned that there were people

00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:09.490
who felt, even though they were

00:58:10.430 --> 00:58:12.560
quite economically disadvantaged,

00:58:12.560 --> 00:58:15.200
they felt that they didn't deserve

00:58:15.200 --> 00:58:19.240
the second check, right?

00:58:19.240 --> 00:58:24.240
And I was curious as to what you gathered

00:58:24.490 --> 00:58:28.840
about that kind of mental
frame those people carried.

00:58:28.840 --> 00:58:32.110
Is that a frame where they felt themselves

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:33.730
that they were a burden on society?

00:58:33.730 --> 00:58:37.640
Was it some kind of, is
this the way in which,

00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:39.140
did it reflect, do you think,

00:58:39.140 --> 00:58:42.473
how they viewed their social status,

00:58:43.580 --> 00:58:47.600
as how they view it or
how society views them,

00:58:47.600 --> 00:58:49.550
and that this is something
they have internalized?

00:58:49.550 --> 00:58:52.690
So I was very curious about people

00:58:52.690 --> 00:58:55.530
and their reactions to what would be seen

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:58.450
as entitlements, right,
social entitlements,

00:58:58.450 --> 00:59:00.293
social safety net entitlements.

00:59:01.710 --> 00:59:03.357
- Yeah, I really appreciate that question.

00:59:03.357 --> 00:59:06.300
And I think it's something
that I myself am grappling with

00:59:06.300 --> 00:59:09.370
how to understand that in
the moment when I heard it.

00:59:09.370 --> 00:59:12.500
And it wasn't endorsed
only by one participant.

00:59:12.500 --> 00:59:14.640
Two other participants in the same group

00:59:14.640 --> 00:59:17.090
endorsed the same view.

00:59:17.090 --> 00:59:20.833
And just to provide a
little bit of context there,

00:59:21.850 --> 00:59:25.920
really, this was a focus
group about poverty.

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:29.900
And so, these were persons who really,

00:59:29.900 --> 00:59:34.570
without the benefits, or
without the stimulus check,

00:59:34.570 --> 00:59:38.200
and without other benefits
that impacted housing,

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:41.450
would find themselves
either without housing

00:59:41.450 --> 00:59:42.730
and on the streets,

00:59:42.730 --> 00:59:46.540
or completely just unable to survive.

00:59:46.540 --> 00:59:51.140
And so, for me, and this is
an active interpretation,

00:59:51.140 --> 00:59:53.270
because in the moment,
it was really difficult

00:59:53.270 --> 00:59:56.930
to understand because it
was just a very fixed idea

00:59:56.930 --> 01:00:00.600
that just receiving,

01:00:00.600 --> 01:00:02.400
and the idea was really articulated

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:05.940
as receiving money from the
government is always bad.

01:00:05.940 --> 01:00:09.620
And I think it was really a clash of ideas

01:00:09.620 --> 01:00:11.620
about the American way of life

01:00:11.620 --> 01:00:15.620
and ideas about what pulling
yourself by the bootstraps

01:00:15.620 --> 01:00:19.060
look like, and all of these kind of tropes

01:00:19.060 --> 01:00:21.510
of independence and of freedom

01:00:22.440 --> 01:00:24.730
that actually clash very directly

01:00:24.730 --> 01:00:29.510
with some direct needs
that people might have,

01:00:29.510 --> 01:00:32.280
or ways in which the government-

01:00:32.280 --> 01:00:33.280
- That reality, right?

01:00:33.280 --> 01:00:34.650
- That reality.
- The reality

01:00:34.650 --> 01:00:35.990
of their lives, right?

01:00:35.990 --> 01:00:37.400
- Yeah, but it is interesting

01:00:37.400 --> 01:00:39.920
that you would stick with the tropes

01:00:39.920 --> 01:00:44.730
and with the myths rather
than with reality itself.

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:46.930
And then, you know, this would be

01:00:46.930 --> 01:00:51.880
an interesting topic of just more inquiry,

01:00:51.880 --> 01:00:54.593
just to understand how
these things interact.

01:00:55.700 --> 01:00:58.930
- And I think that one of
the reasons I picked that up

01:00:58.930 --> 01:00:59.960
from what you were saying is,

01:00:59.960 --> 01:01:02.140
you know, the rhetoric

01:01:02.140 --> 01:01:07.140
with the right wing camp, if you will,

01:01:07.760 --> 01:01:11.330
is that, and the fiscally conservative

01:01:12.390 --> 01:01:16.100
members of, citizens of the country,

01:01:16.100 --> 01:01:18.610
is that somehow the sort of people

01:01:18.610 --> 01:01:22.870
who are on welfare would
just chronically love

01:01:22.870 --> 01:01:24.960
to be on welfare, and they wanna continue

01:01:24.960 --> 01:01:28.550
to be on welfare, and they are
happy to exploit the state.

01:01:28.550 --> 01:01:32.010
So I think this would be very, very useful

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:34.900
to counter that narrative, right?

01:01:34.900 --> 01:01:38.990
That it's not sort of a
uniformly held position.

01:01:38.990 --> 01:01:41.410
There is a sense of dignity
in which, as you said,

01:01:41.410 --> 01:01:43.540
people want to hang onto these tropes

01:01:44.910 --> 01:01:48.800
at the expense of their
own individual needs

01:01:48.800 --> 01:01:49.880
at the time, right?

01:01:49.880 --> 01:01:52.900
So that is sort of what I found

01:01:52.900 --> 01:01:55.710
really sort of intriguing about this.

01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:58.307
And out of curiosity,
where they often people

01:01:58.307 --> 01:01:59.480
who had dependents?

01:01:59.480 --> 01:02:01.310
Were they single mothers who said this,

01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:02.660
or were they single people,

01:02:02.660 --> 01:02:06.150
or is it too hard to
classify a demographic

01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:07.630
and say something?

01:02:07.630 --> 01:02:09.420
- No, I mean, that is one participant

01:02:09.420 --> 01:02:10.970
that I remember very well.

01:02:10.970 --> 01:02:15.970
And this was a married man with
children and grandchildren.

01:02:16.620 --> 01:02:20.870
And yeah, I mean, the other piece is that

01:02:20.870 --> 01:02:25.810
that goes kind of in discourse,

01:02:25.810 --> 01:02:28.370
kind of appeared side by side

01:02:28.370 --> 01:02:31.570
with this idea of a cycle of poverty

01:02:31.570 --> 01:02:33.623
that is impossible to get out of.

01:02:35.260 --> 01:02:37.450
So it was interesting to see that,

01:02:37.450 --> 01:02:40.440
this idea of people
loving being on benefits

01:02:40.440 --> 01:02:43.070
doesn't really square out with the fact

01:02:43.070 --> 01:02:46.840
that people are actually
struggling with benefits.

01:02:46.840 --> 01:02:49.220
And even to come out of benefits,

01:02:49.220 --> 01:02:52.880
it feels like a cycle that
is impossible to break.

01:02:52.880 --> 01:02:56.000
And so, those contradictions,

01:02:56.000 --> 01:02:57.930
I mean, those were really
interesting to see.

01:02:57.930 --> 01:02:59.970
And I think that we saw them also

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:04.540
at the highest political
levels in this country,

01:03:04.540 --> 01:03:07.223
and in my country in
Brazil certainly as well.

01:03:08.380 --> 01:03:10.300
So, yeah, it's an interesting phenomena.

01:03:10.300 --> 01:03:12.290
I hope that someone is looking into that,

01:03:12.290 --> 01:03:14.850
cause I just don't have very good answers

01:03:14.850 --> 01:03:18.843
to understand how that
thinking kind of evolves.

01:03:20.760 --> 01:03:21.900
- Thank you so much.

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:23.820
So there's a question from Gerald Jordan,

01:03:23.820 --> 01:03:27.130
who asked, "Great talk,
really interesting findings.

01:03:27.130 --> 01:03:28.540
And it's amazing how you reached

01:03:28.540 --> 01:03:29.830
beyond academic boundaries

01:03:29.830 --> 01:03:32.390
to work with so many
different kinds of people.

01:03:32.390 --> 01:03:34.600
What do you feel was
the most rewarding part

01:03:34.600 --> 01:03:36.590
of doing this work?"

01:03:36.590 --> 01:03:38.680
- Yeah, and I think this
is a great question.

01:03:38.680 --> 01:03:41.540
I think that, yeah, when you
look at this entire project,

01:03:41.540 --> 01:03:44.830
it seems that we did so
many different things

01:03:44.830 --> 01:03:46.823
and I feel like that was really,

01:03:48.720 --> 01:03:50.500
I think that what was rewarding

01:03:50.500 --> 01:03:52.130
and what I really liked about it

01:03:52.130 --> 01:03:55.230
is that we were able to kind of create

01:03:55.230 --> 01:03:57.780
our path as we went along.

01:03:57.780 --> 01:04:01.030
And so, I think a lot
of it was just building

01:04:01.030 --> 01:04:02.710
from what we were listening

01:04:02.710 --> 01:04:05.330
and then being able to identify

01:04:05.330 --> 01:04:08.980
where we had to go next and then doing it.

01:04:08.980 --> 01:04:10.990
So I think that this responsiveness

01:04:10.990 --> 01:04:14.580
to kind of what we were
seeing in practice,

01:04:14.580 --> 01:04:17.980
it's hardly ever possible
in the academic world,

01:04:17.980 --> 01:04:20.610
where you have to plan everything ahead

01:04:20.610 --> 01:04:23.040
and say what you're
gonna do and then do it.

01:04:23.040 --> 01:04:27.280
So I think that this kind of
flipping of the order of things

01:04:27.280 --> 01:04:31.530
and being able to just
have a very adaptive setup

01:04:31.530 --> 01:04:34.390
was to me interesting.

01:04:34.390 --> 01:04:37.720
And really, I just like
to work with people.

01:04:37.720 --> 01:04:39.580
I think that the other rewarding piece

01:04:39.580 --> 01:04:44.180
is just to have people
who are not academics

01:04:44.180 --> 01:04:46.660
working with us in this group.

01:04:46.660 --> 01:04:49.260
And I think it really pushes the work

01:04:49.260 --> 01:04:52.210
closer to the ground and keeps things

01:04:53.530 --> 01:04:56.670
more relevant, I think.

01:04:56.670 --> 01:04:58.393
So I appreciate the question.

01:05:01.033 --> 01:05:01.940
- And a wonderful talk.

01:05:01.940 --> 01:05:04.070
Of course, we were expecting it to be

01:05:04.070 --> 01:05:05.760
and it really delivered.

01:05:05.760 --> 01:05:07.540
I wanted to know if you
could talk a bit more

01:05:07.540 --> 01:05:09.770
about what you learned
about the human capacity

01:05:09.770 --> 01:05:11.540
for resilience.

01:05:11.540 --> 01:05:13.480
It's a double-edged
sword, as you pointed out,

01:05:13.480 --> 01:05:17.560
because of course it comes
after great suffering.

01:05:17.560 --> 01:05:19.010
The more resilient you are,

01:05:19.010 --> 01:05:21.960
it's probably because you've
suffered that much more.

01:05:21.960 --> 01:05:26.170
But what lessons might
you be able to derive,

01:05:26.170 --> 01:05:29.840
as a psychologist, from people,

01:05:29.840 --> 01:05:31.330
you know, from resiliency,

01:05:31.330 --> 01:05:35.120
and how maybe aspects of resiliency

01:05:35.120 --> 01:05:37.810
might be applied to people

01:05:37.810 --> 01:05:41.810
who find themselves in extremely
difficult circumstances,

01:05:41.810 --> 01:05:45.890
like health problems such as the pandemic.

01:05:45.890 --> 01:05:46.840
- Yeah.

01:05:46.840 --> 01:05:47.994
Thanks, Ty.

01:05:47.994 --> 01:05:51.290
That's a thorny question. (laughs)

01:05:51.290 --> 01:05:53.690
I'll respond the best way I can.

01:05:53.690 --> 01:05:55.550
And it's actually funny that you asked

01:05:55.550 --> 01:06:00.550
because resilience
became kind of a buzzword

01:06:00.640 --> 01:06:04.430
that's even hard to kind
of define right now.

01:06:04.430 --> 01:06:06.110
It's being used in so many ways.

01:06:06.110 --> 01:06:10.700
I think a lot of it, my personal problem

01:06:10.700 --> 01:06:13.960
with this concept is really how it places,

01:06:13.960 --> 01:06:16.760
again, the emphasis on the individual.

01:06:16.760 --> 01:06:21.030
And I think that that's
really why I always,

01:06:21.030 --> 01:06:25.486
whenever I talk about
vulnerability and resilience,

01:06:25.486 --> 01:06:27.310
I always do that with the caveat

01:06:27.310 --> 01:06:31.510
that we really need to find better ways

01:06:31.510 --> 01:06:34.390
to kind of conceptualize these ideas

01:06:34.390 --> 01:06:37.240
and including more societal levels

01:06:37.240 --> 01:06:40.130
and kind of environmental aspects.

01:06:40.130 --> 01:06:42.730
And obviously, the individual
is always in the picture,

01:06:42.730 --> 01:06:43.760
and we don't wanna be like,

01:06:43.760 --> 01:06:46.250
we just don't wanna exclude
the individual either.

01:06:46.250 --> 01:06:48.930
And for someone who's a
clinician, for instance,

01:06:48.930 --> 01:06:52.270
who's, you know, before,
peer suffering, right?

01:06:52.270 --> 01:06:55.740
If you're with a person and
that's what you're seeing,

01:06:55.740 --> 01:06:58.183
that is the most important
thing in the moment.

01:06:59.190 --> 01:07:01.130
So it's not to say that the individual

01:07:01.130 --> 01:07:03.990
is not important, that
suffering doesn't exist.

01:07:03.990 --> 01:07:07.210
But I think that the
field really struggles

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:10.840
with conceptual frameworks

01:07:10.840 --> 01:07:15.840
that actually connect these
multiple levels of the issue.

01:07:16.150 --> 01:07:20.040
And the ways in which we
conceptualize these things

01:07:20.040 --> 01:07:23.350
really impact how we
think about treatment,

01:07:23.350 --> 01:07:24.970
how we think about diagnosis,

01:07:24.970 --> 01:07:27.160
how we think about public policy,

01:07:27.160 --> 01:07:28.700
and how we put together

01:07:28.700 --> 01:07:31.530
and how we set up systems of support,

01:07:31.530 --> 01:07:34.380
and how we change societal structures.

01:07:34.380 --> 01:07:36.480
And so, it's not a naive

01:07:38.500 --> 01:07:40.193
question to ask,

01:07:41.897 --> 01:07:43.827
what is the place of the individual,

01:07:43.827 --> 01:07:44.860
what is the place of society

01:07:44.860 --> 01:07:48.170
and where does suffering
factor into all of this?

01:07:48.170 --> 01:07:50.970
It's actually a question
that I feel the field

01:07:50.970 --> 01:07:53.550
has not done a good job at answering.

01:07:53.550 --> 01:07:57.313
And then the pendulum swings
from one side to the other,

01:07:58.340 --> 01:08:00.730
and that we really have a hard time

01:08:00.730 --> 01:08:03.480
thinking about complex issues

01:08:03.480 --> 01:08:08.020
in ways that we can actually
provide supports for people,

01:08:08.020 --> 01:08:09.960
while also keeping in mind

01:08:09.960 --> 01:08:14.243
the larger context in our work.

01:08:15.720 --> 01:08:17.090
So that's kind of a long-winded,

01:08:17.090 --> 01:08:19.680
a typical academic answer. (laughs)

01:08:19.680 --> 01:08:22.000
A long wind winded response. (laughs)

01:08:22.000 --> 01:08:23.240
- No, thank you though, thank you.

01:08:23.240 --> 01:08:25.460
I mean, if I may follow up a bit,

01:08:25.460 --> 01:08:26.570
you know, one of the things

01:08:26.570 --> 01:08:29.500
that it seems a pitfall perhaps

01:08:29.500 --> 01:08:32.470
of trying to understand

01:08:32.470 --> 01:08:37.027
or derive some broader
benefit from resiliency

01:08:38.180 --> 01:08:42.430
is that, could it lead to a
kind of insouciance, right?

01:08:42.430 --> 01:08:45.640
So someone who has suffered greatly before

01:08:45.640 --> 01:08:49.389
might then say, "Oh, well,
I've been through worse, right?

01:08:49.389 --> 01:08:52.930
And not then take precautions.

01:08:52.930 --> 01:08:56.240
I was wondering, with
the individuals you met,

01:08:56.240 --> 01:08:57.950
did you see some of that,

01:08:57.950 --> 01:09:01.347
and how you might have worked
with them if you did at all,

01:09:01.347 --> 01:09:02.500
or you were just listening

01:09:02.500 --> 01:09:06.910
to how they lived that dichotomy?

01:09:06.910 --> 01:09:08.579
- Yeah, that did come up,

01:09:08.579 --> 01:09:11.163
a lot actually.

01:09:11.163 --> 01:09:15.050
Yes, you know, this, "Bring it on,"

01:09:15.050 --> 01:09:17.140
you know, "I've seen worse."

01:09:17.140 --> 01:09:18.520
And also, some people who said,

01:09:18.520 --> 01:09:20.293
"I didn't care about exposure.

01:09:22.030 --> 01:09:24.706
I didn't think that was a problem for me."

01:09:24.706 --> 01:09:28.110
So that is the flip side of it.

01:09:28.110 --> 01:09:29.380
Yeah, it can make you strong,

01:09:29.380 --> 01:09:34.380
but it can also kind of devastate
your sense of self-worth,

01:09:34.990 --> 01:09:37.530
your sense of self care,

01:09:37.530 --> 01:09:39.453
your ability to care for yourself.

01:09:40.600 --> 01:09:42.700
We heard a lot of that too.

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:46.860
And that is, I think, the flip side

01:09:46.860 --> 01:09:48.950
of the whole resilience conversation

01:09:48.950 --> 01:09:51.900
is that it can actually have dramatic

01:09:51.900 --> 01:09:53.870
and devastating effects for people.

01:09:53.870 --> 01:09:55.187
And even though they're there

01:09:55.187 --> 01:09:57.973
and it seems like
they're powering through,

01:09:57.973 --> 01:10:02.973
actually the experience
itself is just devastating.

01:10:04.030 --> 01:10:05.460
And so, we did see that.

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:08.470
And in that way, this
is why whenever I say

01:10:08.470 --> 01:10:09.870
we see immense resilience

01:10:09.870 --> 01:10:12.740
and we see people are not as vulnerable

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:15.630
as we think they are, at the same time,

01:10:15.630 --> 01:10:17.930
I'm always calling attention

01:10:17.930 --> 01:10:22.190
to this caveat that we
shouldn't be romanticizing

01:10:23.210 --> 01:10:24.633
extreme suffering.

01:10:25.990 --> 01:10:30.750
I mean, it does prepare
people in certain ways

01:10:30.750 --> 01:10:32.280
to endure more suffering,

01:10:32.280 --> 01:10:34.640
but is that really what
we wanna accomplish

01:10:34.640 --> 01:10:36.430
as a society, right?

01:10:36.430 --> 01:10:37.733
It begs that question.

01:10:41.530 --> 01:10:44.670
- So I had another question,

01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:48.590
which is, we're really
sort of trying to assess

01:10:48.590 --> 01:10:53.590
the role of mental health post pandemic.

01:10:54.100 --> 01:10:57.700
Do you see there being a real shift

01:10:57.700 --> 01:11:02.060
in the recognition and the
expansion of this category

01:11:02.060 --> 01:11:03.750
of mental health,

01:11:03.750 --> 01:11:08.070
and what counts as illness

01:11:08.070 --> 01:11:12.660
and what counts as preventative measures,

01:11:12.660 --> 01:11:16.170
has the pandemic,
disruptive as it has been,

01:11:16.170 --> 01:11:19.840
has it fundamentally
shifted anything at all,

01:11:19.840 --> 01:11:24.110
you think, in terms of how we view,

01:11:24.110 --> 01:11:26.220
I mean, I don't think there is anybody

01:11:26.220 --> 01:11:27.690
who survived the pandemic

01:11:27.690 --> 01:11:29.760
without mental health consequences

01:11:29.760 --> 01:11:32.080
of some sort or the other.

01:11:32.080 --> 01:11:36.290
For most of us who are
fortunate, they were manageable,

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:40.110
but it goes without saying that it was

01:11:40.110 --> 01:11:43.090
a period of extremes for all of us, right?

01:11:43.090 --> 01:11:46.650
And has it really shifted
the conversation, Ana?

01:11:46.650 --> 01:11:49.710
I mean, are we going to
see any lasting effects

01:11:49.710 --> 01:11:52.540
of this once in a generation, I mean,

01:11:52.540 --> 01:11:55.493
once in a hundred years sort of pandemic?

01:11:57.631 --> 01:11:59.490
- That's a good question.

01:11:59.490 --> 01:12:03.570
I mean, I don't know that
I can make any predictions

01:12:03.570 --> 01:12:04.520
about-
- No, but just

01:12:04.520 --> 01:12:06.780
your sense of it, your sense of-

01:12:06.780 --> 01:12:10.483
- My feeling is that, so
one thing I feel did happen,

01:12:11.420 --> 01:12:15.403
mental health is the most
talked-about subject.

01:12:17.250 --> 01:12:20.760
I think that I cannot be in a space

01:12:20.760 --> 01:12:23.320
where mental health is not a topic.

01:12:23.320 --> 01:12:25.853
I feel like that's in the
news every single day,

01:12:26.890 --> 01:12:31.410
and that this initial kind of prediction

01:12:31.410 --> 01:12:34.310
of there will be a
tsunami of mental illness,

01:12:34.310 --> 01:12:38.980
that which was said in the
beginning of the pandemic

01:12:38.980 --> 01:12:41.760
was kind of a self-fulfilling prophecy,

01:12:41.760 --> 01:12:46.760
in that by codifying and by understanding

01:12:47.100 --> 01:12:50.260
the reactions that people were having

01:12:50.260 --> 01:12:55.260
to this enormous disruption
as mental health problems,

01:12:56.250 --> 01:13:01.200
I think that by placing it
in that conceptual field

01:13:03.496 --> 01:13:05.570
created a lot of this situation.

01:13:05.570 --> 01:13:07.677
Now, the way we're talking
about our suffering

01:13:07.677 --> 01:13:10.670
and the way we're talking
about what happened to us

01:13:10.670 --> 01:13:12.380
during this time is by saying

01:13:12.380 --> 01:13:14.730
that we're having mental health problems.

01:13:14.730 --> 01:13:17.720
And, you know, that is
true in so many ways.

01:13:17.720 --> 01:13:19.450
I mean, this is the way that we're talking

01:13:19.450 --> 01:13:20.410
about our experience.

01:13:20.410 --> 01:13:24.730
We're not sleeping well, we have anxiety,

01:13:24.730 --> 01:13:27.030
we haven't seen people in two years,

01:13:27.030 --> 01:13:30.750
children haven't left the house in years

01:13:30.750 --> 01:13:32.150
and interact with other kids.

01:13:32.150 --> 01:13:37.150
So all of these things that
happen because of the pandemic,

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:40.360
obviously, are gonna have consequences

01:13:40.360 --> 01:13:44.140
that we don't really anticipate,
we don't really know.

01:13:44.140 --> 01:13:48.270
But the fact that the
conversation is so centered

01:13:48.270 --> 01:13:51.900
in framing, this does concern me slightly

01:13:51.900 --> 01:13:56.070
because it means that the response though,

01:13:56.070 --> 01:13:57.920
how can we think of a response

01:13:57.920 --> 01:14:02.590
outside of the individual treatment realm

01:14:02.590 --> 01:14:06.520
and more related to, how can
we survive this as a society

01:14:06.520 --> 01:14:10.193
and what kind of group
of people do we wanna be?

01:14:11.370 --> 01:14:16.370
I feel like it kind of puts
that question to the side

01:14:17.210 --> 01:14:22.210
when we focus so much
on individual suffering.

01:14:22.590 --> 01:14:24.693
And I also think that for people

01:14:24.693 --> 01:14:27.910
who are being diagnosed
with anxiety, depression,

01:14:27.910 --> 01:14:32.383
or whatever diagnosis
we all might be getting,

01:14:34.390 --> 01:14:36.313
how is that gonna help us,

01:14:37.460 --> 01:14:39.000
and that's a sincere question,

01:14:39.000 --> 01:14:41.840
how is that gonna help us make meaning

01:14:41.840 --> 01:14:44.957
and find a way out of this

01:14:46.539 --> 01:14:48.070
in a more collective way

01:14:48.070 --> 01:14:49.230
rather than-
- Right, right.

01:14:49.230 --> 01:14:52.150
I was gonna say, I mean,
I think the key word here

01:14:52.150 --> 01:14:56.340
that you are bringing to
our attention repeatedly

01:14:56.340 --> 01:15:00.620
is the collective
confrontation of these problems

01:15:00.620 --> 01:15:02.867
rather than delegating
them to the individual.

01:15:02.867 --> 01:15:06.020
I mean of course in America,
there's a very strong

01:15:06.020 --> 01:15:10.440
self-help culture, right,
that predates the pandemic.

01:15:10.440 --> 01:15:14.170
And I can only imagine that we are soon

01:15:14.170 --> 01:15:17.890
going to be seeing lots and
lots of self-help books,

01:15:17.890 --> 01:15:20.030
you know, get out of the pandemic funk,

01:15:20.030 --> 01:15:22.460
help yourself get out
of the pandemic funk,

01:15:22.460 --> 01:15:24.250
and so on and so forth, right?

01:15:24.250 --> 01:15:27.550
So, yeah, I share your anxiety

01:15:27.550 --> 01:15:31.100
that despite the scope of this pandemic,

01:15:31.100 --> 01:15:34.840
it probably did not convert our society

01:15:35.780 --> 01:15:38.820
from being so individualist

01:15:38.820 --> 01:15:43.820
to a more communitarian,
collective vision sort of view.

01:15:44.170 --> 01:15:48.320
And I think that it's sad

01:15:48.320 --> 01:15:50.690
that if this didn't do it,

01:15:50.690 --> 01:15:54.350
what else could really shift us to,

01:15:54.350 --> 01:15:56.720
because I think, as you point out, right,

01:15:56.720 --> 01:16:01.040
there's a way in which inequity

01:16:01.040 --> 01:16:04.420
is sort of embedded in
this individualism, right?

01:16:04.420 --> 01:16:07.390
So, I mean, I'm not gonna
get into the big questions

01:16:07.390 --> 01:16:10.440
of capitalism, and hyper-capitalism,

01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:12.610
and the difference between the individual

01:16:12.610 --> 01:16:16.310
and the social models, east versus west.

01:16:16.310 --> 01:16:17.210
I mean, there've been all these

01:16:17.210 --> 01:16:20.130
sort of simplifying, north versus south.

01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:22.850
People have done a lot of analysis, right,

01:16:22.850 --> 01:16:27.460
trying to argue that there are
some inescapable consequences

01:16:27.460 --> 01:16:30.930
of a capitalist and a
hyper-capitalist society.

01:16:30.930 --> 01:16:35.070
But I think if any tsunami of an event

01:16:35.070 --> 01:16:37.340
could have shifted the conversation,

01:16:37.340 --> 01:16:40.570
shifted the perspective,
it was this pandemic.

01:16:40.570 --> 01:16:45.169
I guess one can be optimistic
and say it's early days, but.

01:16:45.169 --> 01:16:47.080
- Yeah.

01:16:47.080 --> 01:16:48.990
- Are you an optimist about that? (laughs)

01:16:48.990 --> 01:16:52.216
- I couldn't agree with you more, Priya.

01:16:52.216 --> 01:16:56.070
I mean, I feel like I have to
be an optimist and hold hope,

01:16:56.070 --> 01:16:58.903
but I'm also very uncertain.

01:17:00.970 --> 01:17:02.950
Yeah, and I just wanted to acknowledge,

01:17:02.950 --> 01:17:05.260
I didn't know that I could
see who the attendees are,

01:17:05.260 --> 01:17:06.940
but wanted to acknowledge some people

01:17:06.940 --> 01:17:09.740
who actually participated and
were part of our core group,

01:17:09.740 --> 01:17:13.110
Larry Davidson, Claire Bien.

01:17:13.110 --> 01:17:14.563
Gerald is here.

01:17:15.570 --> 01:17:17.330
So I'm just happy that we have colleagues

01:17:17.330 --> 01:17:18.700
in the room as well.

01:17:18.700 --> 01:17:19.533
- Yeah, that's great.

01:17:19.533 --> 01:17:23.020
And, yeah, hey, guys, feel
free to ask any questions.

01:17:23.020 --> 01:17:25.300
I think Ty says she has one more.

01:17:25.300 --> 01:17:28.500
So I feel bad that we
are monopolizing Ana.

01:17:28.500 --> 01:17:31.310
So anyone else, and
it's getting on in time.

01:17:31.310 --> 01:17:35.340
So before we wrap up, if
anyone else has any questions,

01:17:35.340 --> 01:17:37.880
please type them up.

01:17:37.880 --> 01:17:38.970
Meanwhile, Ty, go ahead.

01:17:38.970 --> 01:17:40.100
You said you had one more question.

01:17:40.100 --> 01:17:41.190
- Yeah, sure, thank you.

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:45.470
So you quoted, you offered some
really evocative quotations

01:17:45.470 --> 01:17:47.950
from participants, but I
think the most poignant one,

01:17:47.950 --> 01:17:50.480
in my opinion, was one about the person

01:17:50.480 --> 01:17:53.960
who had suffered from
psychosis, a psychotic episode,

01:17:53.960 --> 01:17:56.957
and that "I really needed,"
I'm misquoting now perhaps,

01:17:56.957 --> 01:17:59.890
"but I really needed other people."

01:17:59.890 --> 01:18:04.160
And, you know, he or she was isolated,

01:18:04.160 --> 01:18:06.390
as the rest of us were
at some point, right,

01:18:06.390 --> 01:18:07.930
especially early on.

01:18:07.930 --> 01:18:10.730
And so, building on what
you were just discussing

01:18:10.730 --> 01:18:13.760
before with Priya, you
probably don't wanna delve

01:18:13.760 --> 01:18:15.770
into the realm of policy,

01:18:15.770 --> 01:18:19.600
but what could it look like,

01:18:19.600 --> 01:18:21.330
were there countries where it maybe,

01:18:21.330 --> 01:18:24.020
or societies where this
didn't happen the same way,

01:18:24.020 --> 01:18:27.160
where, you know, if people are suffering,

01:18:27.160 --> 01:18:30.100
we're gregarious creatures
and we need one another,

01:18:30.100 --> 01:18:35.100
and yet, we became, I guess,
perforce you to so atomize.

01:18:35.240 --> 01:18:37.220
You know, really, I
mean, we weren't allowing

01:18:37.220 --> 01:18:40.310
our children to play with other children.

01:18:40.310 --> 01:18:44.280
And for a long time, we're
still really kind of in this

01:18:44.280 --> 01:18:45.930
and just being so cautious,

01:18:45.930 --> 01:18:49.453
and being, I guess, somehow,

01:18:50.720 --> 01:18:52.890
viewing in the worst instances,

01:18:52.890 --> 01:18:56.110
viewing one another as vectors of disease,

01:18:56.110 --> 01:19:01.110
and kind of being so anxious
about reaching out to others.

01:19:01.460 --> 01:19:05.760
And that, of course, has has
really deleterious effects

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:08.540
on an individual and a society.

01:19:08.540 --> 01:19:12.510
Do you know of places
where that didn't happen

01:19:12.510 --> 01:19:14.137
to that extent, and people were able

01:19:14.137 --> 01:19:16.643
to kind of live a better model of it?

01:19:17.910 --> 01:19:19.750
- I actually, so that is one,

01:19:19.750 --> 01:19:21.890
and I'm curious to know
about that as well.

01:19:21.890 --> 01:19:23.280
And I think we'll know more

01:19:23.280 --> 01:19:26.070
once more research starts coming out.

01:19:26.070 --> 01:19:30.050
But what comparing the
different responses, reactions,

01:19:30.050 --> 01:19:33.700
the different social distancing measures,

01:19:33.700 --> 01:19:34.720
the different shelter in place,

01:19:34.720 --> 01:19:37.910
like, I think it'll take
time for us to understand

01:19:37.910 --> 01:19:40.070
what the right choices were.

01:19:40.070 --> 01:19:43.050
I will say that in
terms of our population,

01:19:43.050 --> 01:19:46.570
the population of people
with psychiatric diagnosis,

01:19:46.570 --> 01:19:49.070
as you saw, that is already clear

01:19:49.070 --> 01:19:51.060
that they were disproportionately,

01:19:51.060 --> 01:19:52.720
and died disproportionately

01:19:52.720 --> 01:19:54.370
compared to the general population.

01:19:54.370 --> 01:19:56.240
So that's something for us to consider,

01:19:56.240 --> 01:19:59.100
that actually a psychiatric diagnosis,

01:19:59.100 --> 01:20:01.910
a serious mental illness
does predict worse outcomes

01:20:02.980 --> 01:20:03.813
for this.

01:20:03.813 --> 01:20:07.370
And we also know that in
terms of access to services,

01:20:07.370 --> 01:20:10.630
people who were receiving services,

01:20:10.630 --> 01:20:12.270
and we had a lot of
people talk about this,

01:20:12.270 --> 01:20:14.260
suddenly stopped receiving services,

01:20:14.260 --> 01:20:18.993
or the services that
replaced the in-person,

01:20:20.927 --> 01:20:24.060
the services that they got
were just not sufficient

01:20:24.060 --> 01:20:25.930
to help them.

01:20:25.930 --> 01:20:28.860
And so, I think there is,

01:20:28.860 --> 01:20:32.040
you know, when I talk
about conceptualizing

01:20:32.040 --> 01:20:35.340
and framing suffering in psychiatric terms

01:20:35.340 --> 01:20:37.843
or in pathologizing poverty,

01:20:38.780 --> 01:20:42.850
I wanna make sure that we
don't forget that people

01:20:42.850 --> 01:20:45.913
are getting services and are
being helped by services,

01:20:49.731 --> 01:20:53.230
and how access to
services actually suffered

01:20:54.070 --> 01:20:55.010
with the pandemic.

01:20:55.010 --> 01:20:58.880
And we saw that with other
health conditions as well,

01:20:58.880 --> 01:21:01.330
that people just didn't
go for their checkups,

01:21:01.330 --> 01:21:03.080
and didn't go for their screenings,

01:21:03.080 --> 01:21:08.080
and how much of a broader
impact in general health

01:21:10.080 --> 01:21:12.143
the whole pandemic had.

01:21:15.500 --> 01:21:16.614
- Thank you.

01:21:16.614 --> 01:21:17.447
- Sure.

01:21:21.080 --> 01:21:23.563
- Let's see if there
are any more questions.

01:21:24.440 --> 01:21:28.066
If not, we should, let
me just wait and see

01:21:28.066 --> 01:21:30.716
if there's anything else
that's come up with the Q&amp;A.

01:21:31.957 --> 01:21:36.360
No, let's thank Ana for a wonderful talk

01:21:36.360 --> 01:21:37.440
and a great project.

01:21:37.440 --> 01:21:40.640
We wish you the best in continuing

01:21:40.640 --> 01:21:43.623
with this very, very
important line of research.

